    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger.jpg" alt="William Pitt the Younger" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OlderPittThe_Younger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Hoppner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland was created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1800">Acts of Union</a> of 1800, passed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-great-britain">Parliament of Great Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a>, which took effect on 1 January 1801. Ireland had been a separate kingdom under the English, then British, crown, with a Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> confined to Protestants, which had won legislative independence in 1782. The rebellion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">Society of United Irishmen</a> in 1798, in which many thousands died and a French force landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">County Mayo</a>, convinced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> that union was needed to secure Ireland during the war with revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Irish Parliament rejected union in 1799 but accepted it in 1800, after the government used peerages, offices and compensation for the owners of parliamentary boroughs to win votes. Ireland received 100 seats in the House of Commons at Westminster and 28 representative peers and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in the House of Lords, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> was united with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. Pitt intended to follow union with Catholic emancipation, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> refused, regarding it as a breach of his coronation oath, and Pitt resigned in 1801. The new state’s flag, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Flag</a>, added the cross of St Patrick to those of St George and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">St Andrew</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway.jpg" alt="Liverpool and Manchester Railway" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway">Liverpool and Manchester Railway</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opening_Liverpool_and_Manchester_Railway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.B. Clayton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom was the first industrial nation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> textiles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, iron and engineering, and from 1830 the railways, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway">Liverpool and Manchester Railway</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the ‘workshop of the world’, as displayed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> of 1851. The population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> grew from about 10.5 million in 1801 to over 40 million in 1911, and by 1851 most people lived in towns. Industrialisation brought crowded cities, child labour and poverty, answered gradually by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/factory-acts">Factory Acts</a>, public health laws, trade unions and, from 1870, state elementary schooling. After the repeal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corn-laws">Corn Laws</a> in 1846 the country embraced free trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> became the centre of world finance, shipping and insurance, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a> on the gold standard. Ireland’s experience was different: apart from the linen and shipbuilding industries around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>, it remained largely agrarian, dependent on tenant farming and, for the poor, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-famine-ireland">Great Famine</a> of 1845–1852, caused by potato blight and worsened by government policy and evictions, killed about a million people and drove another million to emigrate; Ireland’s population fell from about 8.2 million in 1841 to 4.4 million in 1911. Religion divided society, with an Anglican establishment, strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nonconformism">Protestant Nonconformity</a> in Britain and a Catholic majority in Ireland.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trafalgar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-trafalgar.jpg" alt="Battle of Trafalgar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trafalgar">Battle of Trafalgar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Trafalgar_by_William_Clarkson_Stanfield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clarkson Frederick Stanfield, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was the foundation of British power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trafalgar">Battle of Trafalgar</a> in 1805 ended the threat of French invasion, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a>’s campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> and his victory, with Prussian forces, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Battle of Waterloo</a> in 1815 helped bring down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. For a century afterwards the navy policed the seas, hunted slave ships through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa-squadron">West Africa Squadron</a> and backed the opening of markets, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> (1839–1842). The army, small and long-service, was mainly employed in imperial wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and elsewhere, and, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> (1853–1856), whose mismanagement prompted reform; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardwell-reforms">Cardwell reforms</a> of 1868–1874 abolished the purchase of officers’ commissions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> (1899–1902) exposed further weaknesses and led to the creation of a general staff. From 1906 a naval race with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> followed the launch of HMS <em>Dreadnought</em>, the first all-big-gun battleship. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the United Kingdom mobilised millions of men, at first by volunteering and from 1916 by conscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, though never in Ireland; the battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">Somme</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-passchendaele">Passchendaele</a> cost hundreds of thousands of casualties, and more than 700,000 servicemen from the United Kingdom died.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell.jpg" alt="Daniel O'Connell" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_O%27Connell.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Mulrenin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> governed by a Cabinet responsible to Parliament, whose power grew at the expense of the crown. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O’Connell</a>’s mass campaign, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-catholic-relief-act-1829">Roman Catholic Relief Act</a> of 1829 allowed Catholics to sit in Parliament. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">Reform Act</a> of 1832 abolished many rotten boroughs and gave the vote to much of the middle class; further Reform Acts in 1867 and 1884 enfranchised most male householders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballot-act-1872">Ballot Act</a> of 1872 introduced secret voting, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1918">Representation of the People Act</a> of 1918 gave the vote to almost all men over 21 and to most women over 30. Two-party politics developed between Conservatives led by figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a> and Liberals under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-acts-1911-and-1949">Parliament Act</a> of 1911 ended the House of Lords’ absolute veto. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> (r. 1837–1901) reigned as a constitutional monarch, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northcote-trevelyan-report">Northcote–Trevelyan Report</a> of 1854 led to a civil service recruited by competitive examination. Ireland was governed by a Lord Lieutenant and a Chief Secretary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, with an armed police force, the Royal Irish Constabulary; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> was disestablished in 1869, and land acts from 1870 to 1903 gradually transferred land to tenant farmers. Gladstone’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-home-rule-bills">Home Rule Bills</a> of 1886 and 1893 failed, and a third, passed in 1914, was suspended for the war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species.jpg" alt="On the Origin of Species" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Origin_of_Species_title_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Murray, Publisher, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At its height the United Kingdom was the world’s leading industrial, financial and naval power and the centre of the largest empire in history, which by the early 1920s covered about a quarter of the world’s land surface. It spread railways, steam shipping, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and industrial technology across the globe, and its free-trade policy and the gold standard shaped the nineteenth-century world economy. After abolishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in 1807 and, through the Slavery Abolition Act of 1833, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in most of its colonies, it pressed other states to end the trade. Its parliamentary institutions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> were adopted, with variations, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, and English spread as a global language. Science and thought flourished: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a></em> (1859), the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell">James Clerk Maxwell</a> on electromagnetism, and the economics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ricardo">David Ricardo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>. Writers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jane-austen">Jane Austen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronte-sisters">Brontë sisters</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-eliot">George Eliot</a>, W.B. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">Yeats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a> made the period a high point of literature in English. The union with Ireland also left a darker legacy: the Famine and mass emigration created large Irish communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Australia, and the unresolved Irish question shaped politics in both islands throughout the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The union ended in Ireland. Home Rule, demanded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-parliamentary-party">Irish Parliamentary Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stewart-parnell">Charles Stewart Parnell</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-redmond">John Redmond</a>, was opposed by Unionists, concentrated in Ulster, who signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulster-covenant">Ulster Covenant</a> in 1912 and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulster-volunteers">Ulster Volunteers</a>. The Government of Ireland Act 1914 was passed but suspended for the duration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1916 was quickly suppressed, but the execution of its leaders shifted opinion towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a>, which won 73 of Ireland’s 105 seats in the general election of December 1918. Its members met as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a> in January 1919 and declared independence, and the Irish Republican Army fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a> against British forces from 1919 to 1921. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-ireland-act-1920">Government of Ireland Act 1920</a> created separate parliaments for Northern Ireland, which opened in 1921, and Southern Ireland. After a truce in July 1921, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> signed on 6 December 1921 gave twenty-six counties dominion status as the Irish Free State, established on 6 December 1922, while Northern Ireland at once chose to remain in the United Kingdom. The remaining state was formally renamed the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> and Northern Ireland by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-and-parliamentary-titles-act-1927">Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act</a> of 1927. Ireland left the Commonwealth and became a republic in 1949.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, created by the union of 1801, was the first industrial nation and the centre of the largest empire in history, shaping world trade, politics and culture in the nineteenth century, while its failure to reconcile Ireland, above all after the Great Famine, led to Irish independence in 1922.</em></p>

